{
 "exercise": "adv-93",
 "unit": "unit16-advanced",
 "title": "93: FILE Structure Attack",
 "desc": "Goal: Exploit the FILE structure vtable to hijack control flow. In glibc, stdin, stdout, and stderr are _IO_FILE structures containing an internal vtable pointer (__vtable) at a fixed offset. This vtable has entries for __overflow, __underflow, __finish, and other I/O operations. When you call fclose(fp) or fflush(fp), glibc invokes fp->__vtable->__finish(fp) or fp->__vtable->__overflow(fp).Attack: If you can corrupt the _IO_FILE structure (via heap overflow, UAF, or arbitrary write), overwrite the vtable pointer to point at a fake vtable. When fclose() or any I/O operation triggers a vtable call, execution jumps to your controlled address. Note: glibc 2.24+ added IO_validate_vtable to restrict vtable pointers to the __libc_IO_vtables section, but this can be bypassed using _IO_str_jumps or _IO_wstr_jumps.Overflow the buffer and overwrite the return address with win() to model the FILE vtable hijack.",
 "source": [
  "// Simplified _IO_FILE structure",
  "struct _IO_FILE {",
  "    int _flags;          // +0x00",
  "    char *_IO_read_ptr;  // +0x04",
  "    char *_IO_read_end;  // +0x08",
  "    char *_IO_read_base; // +0x0c",
  "    char *_IO_write_base;// +0x10",
  "    char *_IO_write_ptr; // +0x14",
  "    char *_IO_write_end; // +0x18",
  "    char *_IO_buf_base;  // +0x1c",
  "    // ... more fields ...",
  "    void *__vtable;      // +0x94 (32-bit)",
  "};",
  "",
  "// vtable layout:",
  "// __finish, __overflow, __underflow, ...",
  "",
  "void win() { printf(\"FLAG\\n\"); }",
  "",
  "void vuln() {",
  "    char buf[16];",
  "    FILE *fp = fopen(\"/tmp/x\", \"w\");",
  "    gets(buf);",
  "    // Corrupt fp->__vtable via overflow",
  "    fclose(fp);  // triggers __finish!",
  "}"
 ],
